   "xml_name": "PS1_051_MON_Co Prime Check",
   "display_name": "MON_Co Prime Check",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Two positive integers are called <b>co-prime</b> (or \"relatively prime\") if the <b>only</b> positive integer that divides both of them exactly is 1. In other words, they share no common factor bigger than 1.</p>\n<p>For example, 8 and 15 are co-prime: the factors of 8 are 1, 2, 4, 8, and the factors of 15 are 1, 3, 5, 15 - the only number in both lists is 1. But 8 and 12 are <b>not</b> co-prime, because both are divisible by 4 (and by 2).</p>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> you can check every integer from 2 up to the smaller of the two numbers, and see if it divides <b>both</b> numbers exactly (remainder 0 for both). If you find even one such number, they are not co-prime.</p>\n<p>Write a Python program that reads two integers (one per line, each greater than 1) and prints <code>Co-prime</code> if they are co-prime, or <code>Not Co-prime</code> if they are not.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>8\n15</pre> Output: <pre>Co-prime</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> Input: <pre>8\n12</pre> Output: <pre>Not Co-prime</pre></p>",
