   "xml_index": 55,
   "xml_name": "PS1_055_MON_Second Largest In A List",
   "display_name": "MON_Second Largest In A List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>You are given the <b>outline</b> of a Python function that takes a list as input and returns the <b>second largest</b> element in the list. Fill in the missing logic of the function.</p>\n<p>The outline already sets up two variables, <code>largest</code> and <code>second</code>, by comparing the first two elements of the list. Your job is to complete the loop that goes through the <b>rest</b> of the list (from index 2 onwards) and correctly updates <code>largest</code> and <code>second</code> as it goes.</p>\n<p><b>The rule to update them:</b> for each remaining value, if it is bigger than the current <code>largest</code>, then the old <code>largest</code> becomes the new <code>second</code>, and this value becomes the new <code>largest</code>. Otherwise, if it is merely bigger than the current <code>second</code> (but not bigger than <code>largest</code>), it becomes the new <code>second</code>.</p>\n<p>You may assume the list passed in always has at least two elements.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>second_largest([12, 45, 3, 27, 45, 19, 8])</code> should return <code>45</code> (45 is both the largest and, from its second occurrence, correctly the second largest too).</p>\n<p><b>Example 2 (edge case - only two elements):</b> <code>second_largest([10, 20])</code> should return <code>10</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def second_largest(lst):\n    # lst is guaranteed to have at least two elements\n    if lst[0] > lst[1]:\n        largest = lst[0]\n        second = lst[1]\n    else:\n        largest = lst[1]\n        second = lst[0]\n\n    # TODO: loop through the rest of the list (from index 2 onwards)\n    # and update 'largest' and 'second' correctly.\n    # write your code here\n\n\n    return second\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(second_largest([12, 45, 3, 27, 45, 19, 8]))",
