   "display_name": "TUE_Perfect Number Check",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A positive integer is called a <b>perfect number</b> if it is exactly equal to the sum of its <b>proper divisors</b> - that is, all the positive integers smaller than it that divide it exactly (not counting the number itself).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>28</code> is a perfect number: its proper divisors are <code>1, 2, 4, 7, 14</code>, and <code>1+2+4+7+14 = 28</code>. Another example is <code>6</code>: its proper divisors are <code>1, 2, 3</code>, and they add up to <code>6</code>. Most numbers are <b>not</b> perfect - for example, the proper divisors of <code>15</code> are <code>1, 3, 5</code>, which sum to <code>9</code>, not <code>15</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> loop through every integer from 1 up to (but not including) the number itself, check if each one divides the number exactly (remainder 0), and if so add it to a running total. Then compare that total to the original number.</p>\n<p>Write a Python program that reads an integer (greater than 1) and prints <code>Perfect Number</code> if it is a perfect number, or <code>Not a Perfect Number</code> if it is not.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> Input: <pre>28</pre> Output: <pre>Perfect Number</pre></p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> Input: <pre>15</pre> Output: <pre>Not a Perfect Number</pre></p>",
   "answerpreload": "",
   "mark": 2.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "28",
     "expected": "Perfect Number",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "",
     "stdin": "15",
     "expected": "Not a Perfect Number",
     "extra": "",
